#!/usr/bin/env bash
# scripts/test — run this project's automated test suites.
#
# This is the script /audit-project and the loom activity recipes invoke as
# the project's canonical test command, and the gate the v2 harness suites
# hang off. Before bead tla-xme wired it, all 292 harness assertions were
# advisory — commands someone had to remember to run. That is the
# gate-don't-advise failure this script closes.
#
# Usage:
#   bash scripts/test               # ALL suites (default) — ~450 s
#   bash scripts/test --fast        # only the fast tier    — ~30 s
#   bash scripts/test --verbose     # stream every suite's output, not just failures
#   bash scripts/test <substring>   # run only suites whose label or command matches
#   bash scripts/test --list        # print the suite table and exit
#
# Exits 0 only if every selected suite exits 0. Idempotent, offline, no network.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE DEFAULT IS "EVERYTHING", UNLIKE THE SIBLING liza_base/scripts/test
#
# liza_base defaults to a fast tier because its full suite is ~16 min serial.
# Here the full run is 730.6 s wall, measured 2026-10-17, against the ~957 s
# the budgets below sum to, and the fast tier is 25 of the 39 suites but only
# about 6% of the wall clock. Those are two different numbers and the
# sentence used to blur them: a budget is a per-suite ceiling with headroom
# in it, so the sum is always the larger. It skips every
# TLC-heavy gate: the verdict channel, the grader, the vacuity probes, the
# refinement harness, the seeded-bug matrix, the indeterminate census and the
# drill corpus's rc verification. Defaulting to that would make
# canonical_commands.test name a command that runs a tenth of the cost
# and none of the model checking, which is the same class of lie as naming a
# command that fails by design. So the default is the whole thing, and --fast
# is the opt-in for tight iteration.
#
# The share is given in wall clock rather than in assertions on purpose. This
# script does not aggregate an assertion total, so every such figure written
# here has been a hand-count with a shelf life — the suite count alone has
# been three out of date twice (see .claude/rules/dispatched-agents.md). The
# authority on how many suites there are and what they cost is
# `bash scripts/test --list`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TIER SPLIT — the WHOLE registry re-measured 2026-10-13 (bead tla-0eis.3),
#              this box, TLC2 2026.07.31.184830 / tla2tools v1.8.0, 16 cores.
#              NOT an idle box: a 65-minute game JVM was holding about 0.2 of
#              one core throughout, and that is stated rather than hidden.
#              Run-to-run spread was tight enough to bound its effect — the
#              grader read 99.0 / 98.8 / 98.9 s, a 0.2% spread.
#
# Each budget below is the SLOWEST of three back-to-back serial runs, so it is
# the wall clock this box has actually been seen to need, not an average. That
# is deliberate: the budget-drift nudge (below) measures from it, and a nudge
# that trips on ordinary run-to-run jitter gets ignored.
#
# WHY ALL 25 AT ONCE RATHER THAN THE ROWS THAT COMPLAINED. Bead tla-kqry was
# filed over two stale rows on 2026-10-08, corrected to eight the same day,
# then to nine, and its own note settled on "re-measure all twenty-one on a
# quiet box rather than patching rows". Nine of twenty-one over is not nine
# stale numbers, it is a budget set nobody had re-measured since the box or
# the suites changed — and the rows had been taken on four different days
# against trees of four different shapes, so they were never comparable with
# each other in the first place. The three numbers under each row below all
# come from one pass.
