#                                        Slowest of three: 4.441, 4.554,
#                                        4.579
#
#   NOT ATTRIBUTED, and said so rather than papered over. A budget that
#   tracks a measurement is worth keeping; one padded to stop a row
#   complaining is not, and so is one trimmed on a guess.
#     pipefail idiom ban 3.0 ->  1.8 s   DOWN 40% while the suite GREW from
#                                        19 assertions to 24. Standalone
#                                        reads 1.72 s, so it is not an
#                                        in-suite artefact. The 2.9 s reading
#                                        of 2026-10-02 and the 3.5 s of
#                                        2026-10-08 were real on those days
#                                        and nothing in git explains the drop.
#                                        It has now gone back UP to 4.6 on
#                                        2026-10-14 with one assertion
#                                        added, so this row's readings span
#                                        1.72 to 4.6 and no code change
#                                        explains either end. Treat the row
#                                        as noisy rather than as a signal.
#     vacuity gate      80.6 -> 60.0 s   DOWN 26% with test-vacuity.sh
#                                        unchanged since the 80.6 reading of
#                                        2026-09-04. vacuity.sh itself moved
#                                        in 3f6d845, which is a candidate and
#                                        was not verified.
#     chapter snippets  13.6 ->  9.0 s   DOWN 34%, unexplained.
#     refinement        16.8 -> 12.9 s   DOWN 23%, unexplained.
#     v1 verify-puzzle  11.7 ->  9.8 s   DOWN 16%, unexplained.
#
#   The downward moves are the reason this table now records the box's state.
#   Four suites reading 16% to 34% lower, while the grader reads 67% higher,
#   rules out "the box got faster" as a single explanation: a box effect does
#   not move two rows in opposite directions.
#
# WHAT THE BOUNDARY ACTUALLY IS — not "launches a JVM"
#
# It is tempting to say the slow tier is the JVM tier. It is not: `pcal` is a
# java process too (see ~/bin/pcal), and comment-gate.sh forks several of them
# per self-test while still finishing in 2.4 s. JVM startup is fractions of a
# second and does not decide anything. What costs ten seconds and up is
# running TLC's MODEL CHECKER. So the rule is: a suite that model-checks
# belongs in slow. Re-measure and move the boundary if that changes; do not
# guess it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUDGET DRIFT — WHY A WARNING AND NOT A FAILURE
#
# The tier move above sat undetected for as long as it did because nothing
# read the numbers this script was already printing. It prints each suite's
# wall clock next to its recorded budget, so it has the evidence; it just
# never drew a conclusion from it. It does now: a suite that finishes far
# over budget gets an inline marker plus a BUDGET DRIFT block before the
# verdict line.
#
# It WARNS. It does not fail, and it must not be "upgraded" to failing.
#
#   - Exceeding a budget is not incorrectness. Every suite still passed here;
#     the only thing wrong was which tier a row sat in. Failing the gate for
#     it would make `scripts/test` red for a non-defect.
#   - Wall clock is a property of the box, not of the tree. A loaded laptop,
#     a cold page cache, or a CI runner sharing a host can double these
#     numbers with nothing at all wrong. A gate that goes red on those terms
#     is a flaky gate, and a flaky gate gets --no-verify'd or deleted, which
#     costs the real signal too.
#   - CLAUDE.md's gate-don't-advise rule turns on one question: is a human
#     supposed to weigh in? For "did the suite pass", no — that just has to
#     be true, and it is gated, hard, by the exit code. For "is this suite
#     over budget", yes — someone has to decide whether it is drift worth
#     re-tiering or just a busy afternoon. That is the nudge shape, not the
#     gate shape. What gate-don't-advise forbids is leaving it as a check
#     nobody runs; printing it unconditionally in the normal output, every
#     run, is what satisfies the rule.
#
# THE MARGIN, and why it is where it is.
#
#   Trip when BOTH hold:  elapsed >= 3.0x budget  AND  elapsed - budget >= 5 s
#
#   The multiple is tied to the tier boundary itself. The fast tier tops out
#   at 2.4 s and the slow tier floors at 10.2 s, so any suite that crosses
#   from fast to slow does so by at least 4.2x — comfortably above 3.0x. A
#   3x threshold therefore catches every possible tier crossing by
#   construction, while sitting far enough above the 8% measured jitter, and
#   above the ~2x a genuinely contended box costs, that it stays quiet when
#   nothing has actually changed. The drift this bead fixed was 16.7x.
#
#   The 5 s absolute floor is what makes it usable on the fast tier. A
#   multiple alone is meaningless at sub-second scale: pipefail's 0.7 s
#   budget is 3x'd by any hiccup, and warning that a suite took 2.1 s
#   instead of 0.7 s is noise. Requiring five real seconds of overrun
#   suppresses that completely without weakening the tier-crossing case,
#   since crossing into slow means landing at 10 s or more.
#
#   Both constants are BUDGET_DRIFT_FACTOR and BUDGET_DRIFT_FLOOR_MS below.
#   There is deliberately no environment override: a nudge you can switch
#   off from the caller is a nudge that gets switched off.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set -euo pipefail

SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
REPO_ROOT="$(cd -- "${SCRIPT_DIR}/.." && pwd)"
cd "${REPO_ROOT}" || exit 1

# Fail closed on a mis-resolved root. Every suite path below is relative, so a
# wrong root does not error out — it reports ten separate rc=127 "No such file"
# failures and buries the actual cause. Observed while testing this script's
# own failure path from a copy placed one directory up.
if [ ! -d "${REPO_ROOT}/harness" ] || [ ! -d "${REPO_ROOT}/scripts" ]; then
  echo "scripts/test: ${REPO_ROOT} is not the tla-puzzles root (no harness/ + scripts/)." >&2
  exit 2
fi

# tier | budget-seconds | label | command
#
# budget-seconds is MEASURED wall clock, not an aspiration — the slowest of
# three serial runs on the box named in the header. The tier column is derived
# from it. Keep the rows sorted by budget within each tier.
