  # walked cannot pass as clean, and plants its own positive and negative
  # controls.
  #
  # WHAT IT GUARDS. TLC 2026.07.31.184830 breaks the contract its own bundled
  # Naturals.tla states. `-17 \div 5` comes back -3, so `5 * -3 + 3` is -12
  # rather than -17: the quotient truncates toward zero where the contract
  # forces it to round down, while the remainder half holds. This corpus
  # takes its verdicts from exit codes, so an item written over a negative
  # dividend bakes TLC's bug as the answer. It caught three of the first 80
  # level 0 swipe items, and those three were replaced.
  #
  # IT IS NOT THE TRIPWIRE. The \div assertion in the level 0 swipe row below
  # says the identity is STILL broken and reddens when TLC is FIXED. That one
  # watches the jar. This one watches what authors write, by holding the tree
  # to harness/div-sites.tsv: 76 reviewed sites over 83 occurrences, each row
  # carrying the reason its dividend is safe. "The dividend can never be
  # negative" is not decidable by scanning, so the gate does not try. "A \div
  # nobody has read" is, and that is enough to stop an author.
  #
  # WHEN IT GOES RED, GO AND READ THE DIVIDEND. The gate names the file and
  # the expression. Work out whether that dividend can go negative, then
  # either fix the item or add a row to harness/div-sites.tsv saying why it is
  # safe. NEVER add a row to quiet the gate. A row nobody read is worth
  # nothing, and somebody having looked is the whole content of this check.
  #
  # The other three reds and what each one means. An inventory row matching
  # nothing is a deleted site or a RENAMED one, so look for the rename before
  # deleting the row. A count out of step means a second occurrence landed
  # under a window somebody already reviewed, and it still wants reading. A
  # root floor failing is the one red that is about this gate rather than
  # about the corpus: the scan stopped walking that root.
